You are asked to write the literature review section of a PhD thesis about human evaluation in NLG. Please produce a long section in Latex, referencing all the relevant sources.

The section should include:
- What is human evaluation and why do we need it (compared to automatic evaluation)
- The parameters of human evaluation: e.g. quality criteria, evaluation framework, design (cite belz2020disentangling)
- Types of human evals: Intrinsic vs. extrinsic (cite gkatzia-mahamood-2015-snapshot)
- Limitations, difficulties, and tradeoffs of human eval (cite howcroft2020twenty,kiritchenko-mohammad-2017-best, thomson-reiter-2020-gold, schoch-etal-2020-problem)
- Might be worth mentioning the multiple crowdworkers vs single expert problem, and how in a complex domain such as healthcare experts are a requirement. (cite snow-etal-2008-cheap)
Here are some useful references that should be mentioned in the section:
\begin{itemize}

 \item \citet{mani2002summac}: \blue{SUMMAC: a text summarization evaluation}

 \item \cite{REITER200341} \blue{Lessons from a failure: Generating tailored smoking cessation letters}

 \item \citet{nenkova2004evaluating}: \blue{Evaluating content selection in summarization: The pyramid method}

 \item \citet{dorr2005methodology}: \blue{A Methodology for Extrinsic Evaluation of Text Summarization: Does ROUGE Correlate?}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item they present an extrinsic gold standard protocol for evaluating usefulness of automatic summarisation. \red{They propose the Relevance-Prediction measure [...]}
 \end{itemize}

 \item \citet{choi2005peer}: \blue{A Catalog of Biases in Questionnaires}

 \item \citet{dang2005overview}: \blue{Overview of DUC 2005}

 \item \citet{belz2006comparing}: \blue{Comparing Automatic and Human Evaluation of NLG Systems}

 \item \citet{belz2008intrinsic} \blue{Intrinsic vs. Extrinsic Evaluation Measures for Referring Expression Generation}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item “a system’s ability to produce human-like outputs may be completely unrelated to its effect on human task-performance.”
 \end{itemize}


 \item \cite{sheng2008get} \blue{Get another label? improving data quality and data mining using multiple, noisy labelers}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item overall data quality can actually be improved by having more redundant annotations 
 \end{itemize}

 \item \cite{snow-etal-2008-cheap} \blue{Cheap and fast – but is it good? evaluating non-expert annotations for natural language tasks.}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item a small number of crowdworkers can replace a single expert rater 
 \end{itemize}

 \item \cite{portet2009automatic} \blue{Automatic generation of textual summaries from neonatal intensive care data}


 \item \citet{belz-kow-2010-comparing} \blue{Comparing Rating Scales and Preference Judgements in Language Evaluation}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item argue that rating scales like those used in direct assessments (i.e., evaluating a generated text alone, without referencing other candidates) have many issues: they are unintuitive, agreement numbers are low, and most statistical measures are inappropriate for ordinal data. They find that these issues can be addressed to some extent by switching to preferential judgments
 \end{itemize}

 \item \citet{gkatzia-mahamood-2015-snapshot}: \blue{A snapshot of NLG evaluation practices 2005 - 2014}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item found that about 75\% of published NLG systems rely on intrinsic evaluations with the fraction increasing.
 \end{itemize}

 \item \citet{kiritchenko-mohammad-2017-best} \blue{Best-Worst Scaling More Reliable than Rating Scales: A Case Study on Sentiment Intensity Annotation}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item demonstrated that best-worst scaling (asking evaluators to choose the best and the worst items in a set) is an efficient and reliable method for collecting annotations, and this approach has been used to collect comparative evaluations of generated text
 \end{itemize}

 \item \cite{thomson-reiter-2020-gold} \blue{A gold standard methodology for evaluating accuracy in data-to-text systems}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item high agreement comes with a cost: training, time, money, evaluator selection, etc.
 \end{itemize}

 \item \cite{schoch-etal-2020-problem}: \blue{``This is a Problem, Don’t You Agree?” Framing and Bias in Human Evaluation for Natural Language Generation}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item discuss the role cognitive biases can play in the way researchers elicit human evaluations, such as using positive or negative framing (e.g., How much more fluent is sentence A vs. sentence B?)
 \end{itemize}

 \item \citet{belz2020disentangling}: \blue{Disentangling the properties of human evaluation methods: A classification system to support comparability, meta-evaluation and reproducibility testing.}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item propose a classification system for evaluations based on disentangling (i) what is being evaluated (which aspect of quality), and (ii) how it is evaluated in specific (a) evaluation modes and (b) experimental designs.
 \end{itemize}

 \item \citet{howcroft2020twenty}: \blue{Twenty years of confusion in human evaluation: Nlg needs evaluation sheets and standardised definitions.}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item present (i) our dataset of 165 NLG papers with human evaluations, (ii) the annotation scheme we developed to label the papers for different aspects of evaluations, (iii) quantitative analyses of the annotations, and (iv) a set of recommendations for improving standards in evaluation reporting.
 \item conclude that due to a pervasive lack of clarity in reports and extreme diversity in approaches, human evaluation in NLG presents as extremely confused in 2020, and that the field is in urgent need of standard methods and terminology.
 \end{itemize}

 \item \citet{duvsek2020evaluating}: \blue{Evaluating the state-of-the-art of End-to-End Natural Language Generation: The E2E NLG challenge}

 \item \citet{VANDERLEE2021101151}: \blue{Human evaluation of automatically generated text: Current trends and best practice guidelines}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item Likert scales were the most popular method for rating generated text, used in 56\% of studies (82/147)
 \item only 23\% of NLG papers report statistical analyses
 to determine the significance of their results, and
 only 13\% explicitly state their hypotheses
 \end{itemize}

 \item \cite{rashkin2021measuring}: \blue{Measuring Attribution in Natural Language Generation Models}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item define a framework for evaluating whether generated text is attributable to identified sources.
 \end{itemize}

 \item \citet{pagnoni2021understanding} \blue{Understanding Factuality in Abstractive Summarization with FRANK: A Benchmark for Factuality Metrics}
 \begin{itemize}
 \item introduce and collect annotations for an alternative typology of factual errors which involves fine-grained categories such as Coreference Error and Out of Article Error.
 \end{itemize}

 \item \citet{shimorina2021human}: \blue{The human evaluation datasheet 1.0: A template for recording details of human evaluation experiments in nlp}

\end{itemize}

